  CODE — the MIT License below.
    Everything that builds or runs the site and apps: site/ (Astro app, build
    tools), app/ (Capacitor/iOS shell, release tooling), scripts/,
    .github/workflows/, and configuration files.

  CONTENT — Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0).
    The wiki itself: every markdown page under the category directories
    (benefits/, rights/, crisis/, housing/, es/, …) and the images that
    accompany them. See LICENSE-CONTENT.

The split is deliberate: MIT reads oddly applied to prose, and Creative
Commons is a poor fit for source code. If you are reusing wiki pages —
reprinting a hotline list, translating a benefits guide — the license you
want is LICENSE-CONTENT, and reuse is encouraged.

Neither license is a warranty or a disclaimer. Both are permissions to copy.
Nothing here is legal, medical, or financial advice; see the site's own
notices.

